Amazon API Gateway
Front door for any incoming requests (e.g., a customer or agent starts a CoM via a portal/app).
Amazon SNS (Simple Notification Service) → Pub/Sub messaging  "Fan-out" events: One thing happens → many services get notified instantly.  
Reliable delivery, even if a service is busy or down temporarily.

Amazon SQS (Simple Queue Service) → Queues (especially FIFO for order-critical steps)  Buffers messages so services process at their own speed.  
Automatic retries if something fails (e.g., external system like CSS is slow).  
Dead-letter queues catch permanent failures for manual review.

AWS Lambda  Lightweight, quick functions for simple steps (e.g., validate data, transform events, call small APIs).  
Runs only when needed → cheap and fast.

AWS Step Functions  Orchestrates long/complex workflows (the "sagas").  
Handles sequences like: Step 1 → if OK then Step 2 → if fail then rollback/compensation.  
Can run for hours/days (e.g., waiting for manual approval or external response).  
Built-in error handling, retries, timeouts.

Databases  Amazon DynamoDB (fast NoSQL) → for quick reads/writes of temporary state, customer snapshots, event logs.  
Amazon RDS (PostgreSQL) → for core transactional data that needs strong consistency (bills, contracts).  
Event sourcing/CQRS in places: Store "what happened" as events, build current view on-the-fly.

Security & Observability  Amazon Cognito + IAM roles → secure auth and least-privilege access.  
Encryption everywhere (at rest + in transit).  
Amazon CloudWatch + AWS X-Ray → logging, metrics, tracing every step for audits and debugging.  
Full audit trails for Ofgem/regulatory compliance.

Main Types of Events (in simple terms)Events are just short messages saying "something happened". Examples in a CoM flow:CustomerInitiatedCoM → "User requested switch from credit to prepayment"
CreditCheckRequested → Sent to credit service
CreditCheckCompleted → With pass/fail + details
MeterReadRequested → To smart meter/ECOES service
MeterReadReceived → Actual reading arrived
SwitchValidated → All checks passed
BillingAdjustmentCalculated → New bill/debt/credit amounts
MarketSwitchSubmitted → Sent to CSS (central switching service)
SwitchConfirmedByMarket → CSS says "approved"
CoMCompleted → Final success → trigger welcome email, final bill
FailureDetected → e.g., "Timeout waiting for meter read" → triggers rollback events like CompensationAdjustmentNeeded, NotifyCustomerOfDelay

How It Flows (high-level example)API Gateway receives request → triggers Lambda → publishes CustomerInitiatedCoM event to SNS.
SNS fans out to multiple SQS queues (one per interested microservice).
Each service picks up event from its queue → does its job → publishes next event(s) (success or failure).
For long sequences (e.g., wait for external CSS response): Step Functions coordinates the steps, waits, retries, or runs compensation if needed.
All events logged → DynamoDB for fast queries, CloudWatch for monitoring.

This setup makes the system fast (async, no waiting), resilient (retries, decoupling), scalable (handles thousands of switches/day), and auditable (every step traceable). It avoids the old problem of one slow external call blocking everything.

